abstract: >-
  A formal-methods study of governed computation: how evidence, authority,
  custody, spend, history, refusal, and obligation constrain justified
  conclusions and state transitions. V16 adds Governed Transition Boundaries:
  a generic explicit-factorization core and a bounded evidence surface
  answering, for selected targets and selected source views, whether one total
  decoder recovers the target from the view correctly for every source. Four
  axiom-free core results establish composition, implication of fibre
  constancy, blocking by a target-distinguishing collision, and
  non-restoration by deterministic postprocessing; one declared
  seven-coordinate finite language carries a unique least target-determining
  selection; and five separately scoped witnesses cover fixed-policy
  authorization refusal, selected-context validation, bounded capacity
  realizability, occurrence-link observation, and modeled hidden-relation
  nonidentifiability. The generic statements are standard
  function-factorization and view-determinacy facts and the finite result is
  an exhaustive dependency calculation in a declared table; the contribution
  is their mechanically checked synthesis, and no novelty or priority is
  claimed. V16 promotes no stable surface. The V15 Cross-Calculus Atlas
  remains authoritative for selected edges from Governed Transport, Execution
  Custody, and Continuity Admission, retaining their native judgment indices,
  local countermodels, and exact semantic receipts; those receipt types are
  calculus-specific and not inherently cryptographic. Its classification
  remains ATLAS: no shared bridge algebra, generic frontier composition,
  generic ownership, generic context transport, or universal calculus is
  established. This is not
  specifically a blockchain, smart-contract, legal-protocol, legal-evidence,
  zero-knowledge, audit-log, category-theory, generic state-machine, or
  alternate-reality project. No runtime conformance, JCP implementation,
  operational AG/NQ realization, general authorization theory, temporal
  validity, causal attribution, attestation correctness,
  cryptographic unforgeability, phenomenology, consciousness,
  successful-prediction, or personal-identity claim is made. The DOI above is
  the concept DOI: it identifies the software series, resolves to the latest
  published version, and never changes across releases. Each release's
  version DOI is minted by its Zenodo deposit and recorded there; it is never
  substituted for the concept DOI in this file.
